  "typecheckAfterFollowUp": true,
  "summary": "I built the pendulum clock in src/main.ts. Typecheck and `vite build` pass, and the last render drew with no errors. I checked only still screenshots, so I haven't watched the pendulum swing, the second hand tick or the weights sink. Those follow from the code. The frame also hasn't been checked against the real clock time.\n\n- **Clock:** a tall wooden case with a plinth, a glass-fronted trunk and a hood with a crown, built from boxes, cylinders and spheres. The dial has a brass bezel, a cream face, 12 hour marks and hour, minute and second hands.\n- **Hands:** all three are set from `new Date()` local time. The second hand ticks once per second, and the minute and hour hands move continuously.\n- **Pendulum:** it swings with a 2-second period on a sine curve, so it is slowest at the extremes. It is driven by `Date.now()`, so it crosses the centre exactly on each second tick. Behind it, a 30-tooth escape wheel advances one tooth per second and an anchor rocks with the pendulum.\n- **Weights:** two brass weights on chains sink at 0.003 m/s (the right one slightly slower) and stop after 0.45 m, about 2.5 minutes after page load.\n- **Setting:** warm interior-style lighting from the `interior-warm` look plus three point lights, a wood floor, a back wall, skirting and a rug. The camera is fixed in front, slightly above centre, with the whole clock in view.\n- **Rendering contract:** it draws into `#app`, and `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frame. The engine's own loop drives the frames, so I did not write a separate `requestAnimationFrame` loop.\n\nIn the last render the hour marks are faint against the dial. The renderer also logs two harmless warnings: the glass pane falls back to casting an opaque shadow, and the rug's fabric normal texture is ignored."
}